-- Issues 155 / 156: the uPER encoding of an OBJECT IDENTIFIER / RELATIVE-OID (X.691 23 / 24) is a general length
-- (10.9: one octet below 128, two octets 10xxxxxx from 128) and the subidentifiers of X.690 8.19; at most 20 arcs of
-- up to 64 bits, i.e. 190 (OID) / 200 (RELATIVE-OID) contents octets. The decoders accept exactly what the encoders
-- write: a two-octet length below 128, a fragment header (no value of at most 20 arcs has 16K contents octets), a
-- length of 0, a length that ends inside a subidentifier and an arc beyond 64 bits are rejected. The ACN encoding of
-- these types is the uPER one.
UPER-WIRE-OID DEFINITIONS AUTOMATIC TAGS ::= BEGIN
    T ::= OBJECT IDENTIFIER
    R ::= RELATIVE-OID
    S ::= SEQUENCE { o OBJECT IDENTIFIER, tail INTEGER (0..255) }
END
